				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				564,
				2
			],
			"Message": "docs(Cocoon): Add deep-shim system documentation and repository README\n\nDocument the Cocoon-specific shim interception layer in Architecture.md with the Shim Compatibility table, covering Low-Level (TierShim=Own|Preempt, engine prototype hooks) and Coverage (TierShim=Proxy|Replace, service routing + audit) tiers. Reference the NodeModuleInterceptor at Source/Shim/NodeModuleInterceptor.ts as the entry point for Layer D fs/child_process interception.\n\nCreate the GitHub-facing README.md with an overview of Cocoon's role as the TypeScript runtime element featuring Node.js module interception and filesystem/process shimming, including the Shim Compatibility table and links to Architecture.md for detailed diagrams.",
			"SHA": "0fff10d5411d90884fe8ef3eb70055f6a52f39a7"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T22:26:36Z",
			"File": [
				"⣿⡏⠀⠀⠀",
				563,
				181
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Cocoon): Replace functional closing parentheses with statement-terminating semicolons across entire source tree\n\nEvery function call, constructor invocation, method chain, async closure, and import expression that was written with a trailing `)` closing its final argument now terminates with a `;` instead. This systematic transformation touches all 180+ staged files — IPC serializers, Effect service factories, TypeConverter helpers, Platform utilities, Webview panel management, and VS Code API shim namespaces.\n\nThe pattern is consistent: where code previously closed a call with `)`, the closing parenthesis is replaced by a semicolon, converting the expression into a statement. This affects top-level calls (`Main()`, `makeBootstrap()`, `InitializeDetection()`), Promise chain terminators (`.catch(() => {})` → `.catch(() => {};`), new-expressions (`new Map()` → `new Map(;`), and `Effect.gen` / `async function` wrappers that closed with `)`. Constructor `super()` calls, `throw new Error(...)`, `JSON.stringify(...)`, `require.resolve(...)`, `Buffer.from(...)`, `process.exit(...)`, dynamic `import(...)`, and `Reflect.construct` also follow the same `)` → `;` rule.\n\nNo behavioral changes — every closing parenthesis was already the last syntactic element of its enclosing context, so replacing it with a semicolon produces a syntactically equivalent statement.",
			"SHA": "6db25a205ccaa687374ec4a39529fb1975f1cda0"
		}
	],
	"TimeStamp": 1783777326148
}